I wanted to draw output of secondary Camera3D on MeshInstance3D (PlaneMesh).

<details>
<summary>Scene layout is really simple</summary>

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/17745136/166444271-bbad8926-7fcd-4636-a6b9-0b490336bf93.png)

</details>

<details>
<summary>I wrote simple shader that maps input texture onto plane based on screen UV.</summary>

```glsl
shader_type spatial;
render_mode unshaded;

uniform sampler2D viewport_texture : hint_albedo;

void fragment() {
	vec4 n_out4p0 = texture(viewport_texture, SCREEN_UV);
	ALBEDO = vec3(n_out4p0.xyz);
}
```
</details>

And then I assigned this shader to `OutputPlane` as a `ShaderMaterial` and set that single shader param as a `ViewportTexture` pointing to my `SubViewport`.

<details>
<summary>But all I've got it is a pink texture both in editor and in-game</summary>

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/17745136/166444819-e5e4c00f-17c1-4a67-a7c0-be10f57a27ed.png)

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/17745136/166444862-b3de15b9-35df-43f5-97e9-00d4e43631ef.png)

</details>

I've managed to make it work, but it requires all conditions to be met:

1. SubViewport must have both `size` and `size_2d_override` to be set;
2. SubViewport must be `render_target_update_mode = ALWAYS`;
3. OutputPlane's `viewport_texture` must be grabbed with `viewport.get_texture()` directly from viewport (done via script attached to plane).

<details>
<summary>This is how viewport texture is assigned</summary>

```gdscript
extends MeshInstance3D

@export var viewport: NodePath;
@onready var _viewport = get_node(viewport)

func _ready():
	var m: ShaderMaterial = mesh.surface_get_material(0);
	m.set_shader_param("viewport_texture", _viewport.get_texture())

```

</details>

Note: w/o 3rd workaround (i.e. when shader's `viewport_texture` assigned from editor) shader works properly in editor, but still shows pink color in-game. Also 3rd workaround is not required when `SubViewport` is placed in `SubViewportContainer`.
